One thing I've never quite understood about the firearms people try to
add to game mechanics is the whole instakill concept that always seems
to go with it. What I want to know is how being hit in the head by a
small piece of ballistic metal is so much more lethal than being hit in
the head with a long, heavy piece of metal, swung with the expertise of
a trained warrior. Okay, the bullet can't have a "weak" swing, but
really, a hit to the throat will render most and things completely
incapable of combat and will most likely kill them if they do not
recieve immediate medical care.
Okay, so I guess you could argue that the stuff you're fighting has a
chance to dodge and parry the blows of a weapon and maybe "roll with
them" etc. You might even argue that that's what "hit points" represent.
An ability to avoid lethal blows. But then we have to look at arrows and
crossbows and other ranged weapons. You can't dodge an arrow. You take
an arrow to the eye you're just as dead as if you'd taken a bullet. So
really, every weapon should have an instakill option, but only the
vorpal blade and similar have that. Why?
In my opinion the hitpoint system is pretty flawed. I don't care how
many monsters youlve killed or how good you are at weilding a sword,
there is no logical reason why you should be able to withstand more
direct hits from fiery blasts, or lightning-bolts. This is because the
hit point system is not based on logic. It's based on... Well...
Cinematics, I guess. The more things they kill, the tougher adventurers
get. The concept of experience points makes sense on some level. It's
just very hard to translate that into an in-game effect. There are some
roleplaying games who try to implement realistic combat mechanics, and
there are even some who succeed. But for the old school hack and slash
dungeon adventures the hit point system gives you a very noticable and
real feeling of gaining experience. It's not realistic, but it *feels*
right.
So when the entire system of hit-points and damage is so flawed to begin
with, why try and impose realistic mechanics for a single weapon when
the rest of them are still using the old and unrealistic system?
I guess it's a genre thing. In the movies and stories and book where the
"normal" weapons belong, fights are something that go on and on. You
thrust, riposte, parry, scratches and cuts are delivered in long,
drawn-out battles. The only adverse effect you can suffer in these
battles is death. Flesh-wounds, damaged libs, losing your eyes, all
these things aren't in the system because we all know the heroes of
these stories battle on just as well (if not not better) no matter how
many bleeding wounds they have.
The guns, however, come from another genre altogether. The sixshooter,
at least, has its home in cowboy movies, where the first shot is the
final one, be it in a one-on-one duel or in a massive fight where
indians or "bad cowboys" or whatever fall like flies every time the
hero's gun cracks. So we want our medieval swashbuckling combat, because
we think that's cool, AND the cowboy instakill figthing, because that's
cool too. But the mechanics of the two genres don't go together, not
when we're aiming for recreating the cinematics (not sure that's the
right word) of the genres they imitate. Because that's what the systems
do. They imitate the stories, so we can put ourselves in the action we
see on screen and pages.
I could go on to talk a bit about the newer gunslinger-movies, where
heroes and villains alike can take hit after hit without needing medical
attention, and how systems like d20 modern have translated this in to
action-movie roleplaying systems that use hitpoints almost like the old
D&D and its relatives do. Oh. I think I just did. Anyway, rant over.
